WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.495
Performance tuning begins with a repeatable workload.

00:00:03.495 --> 00:00:09.047
Record the model, quantization, prompt, context setting, runtime version, and acceleration backend.

00:00:09.047 --> 00:00:16.655
Then measure time to first token, prompt processing speed, generation tokens per second, and total completion time.

00:00:16.655 --> 00:00:25.085
Tokens per second describes generation throughput, but it does not capture a long delay while the prompt is processed.

00:00:25.085 --> 00:00:30.637
Both measurements affect whether a coding assistant or chat interface feels responsive.

00:00:30.637 --> 00:00:35.366
Watch GPU utilization and VRAM allocation while the request runs.

00:00:35.366 --> 00:00:42.562
Low GPU activity may indicate CPU execution, insufficient offload, or a workload that is waiting elsewhere.

00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:48.936
VRAM near its limit can produce allocation failures or force compromises in context size.

00:00:48.936 --> 00:00:57.366
Monitor system RAM and storage activity as well; heavy swapping can make a configuration technically functional but operationally unusable.

00:00:57.366 --> 00:01:02.096
Thermals, fan noise, and power draw matter during sustained use.

00:01:02.096 --> 00:01:08.058
Observe temperatures and clock speeds over a long session, not only during startup.

00:01:08.058 --> 00:01:12.376
Falling clocks combined with rising temperature indicate thermal throttling.

00:01:12.376 --> 00:01:18.339
Sudden shutdowns or instability may point to power, cabling, overclocking, or cooling problems.

00:01:18.339 --> 00:01:25.536
Prefer a stable power profile over a marginal performance gain that increases heat and noise substantially.

00:01:25.536 --> 00:01:28.620
Change one setting at a time.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:37.873
GPU offload, batch size, context length, and runtime-specific cache options can improve one phase while worsening another or increasing memory use.

00:01:37.873 --> 00:01:43.424
Keep a small benchmark table and compare results against the real workload.

00:01:43.424 --> 00:01:47.331
The objective is not the highest isolated number.

00:01:47.331 --> 00:01:54.939
It is predictable latency, adequate output quality, and sustained operation within the machine's thermal and memory limits.
